To compute the transpose of a matrix represented as a list of rows (each row being a list), we need to convert columns into rows. This can be done by grouping the i-th element of each row for every valid column index i. Using `zip(*matrix)` is a Pythonic and efficient way to achieve this, as it unpacks the rows and zips their elements together. Edge cases include an empty input list (returning `[]`) and a matrix with rows of length zero (e.g., `[[], []]`), which should return an empty list since there are no columns to transpose.

