To implement `deepClone(v)`, I will use a recursive approach that handles different types of values: primitives (returned as-is), functions (cloned by reference since they’re typically immutable in behavior), arrays, and plain objects. I’ll use a `WeakMap` to track already-cloned objects to prevent infinite recursion on circular references. Edge cases include `null`, `Date`, `RegExp`, `Map`, `Set`, and other built-in types — for simplicity and correctness, I’ll support only plain objects, arrays, `Date`, `RegExp`, `Map`, and `Set` explicitly; other objects (e.g., custom classes) will be cloned as plain objects unless they have a `clone` method (which we won’t assume). Circular references are handled via the `WeakMap`.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function deepClone(v, seen = new WeakMap()) {
  if (v === null || typeof v !== 'object') return v;
  if (seen.has(v)) return seen.get(v);

  let result;
  if (Array.isArray(v)) {
    result = [];
  } else if (v instanceof Date) {
    result = new Date(v);
  } else if (v instanceof RegExp) {
    result = new RegExp(v.source, v.flags);
  } else if (v instanceof Map) {
    result = new Map();
  } else if (v instanceof Set) {
    result = new Set();
  } else {
    result = Object.create(Object.getPrototypeOf(v));
  }

  seen.set(v, result);

  if (v instanceof Map) {
    for (const [key, val] of v) {
      result.set(deepClone(key, seen), deepClone(val, seen));
    }
  } else if (v instanceof Set) {
    for (const val of v) {
      result.add(deepClone(val, seen));
    }
  } else if (Array.isArray(v)) {
    for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
      result[i] = deepClone(v[i], seen);
    }
  } else {
    for (const key in v) {
      if (Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(v, key)) {
        result[key] = deepClone(v[key], seen);
      }
    }
  }

  return result;
}

module.exports = deepClone;
```

