I'll implement `formatBytes(n)` to format byte values into human-readable strings using binary units (1024-based). The function will handle edge cases like negative numbers (returning '0 B'), exact powers of 1024 (to avoid unnecessary decimals), and values below 1024 (displayed as whole bytes). For values ≥1024, I'll use one decimal place only when needed (e.g., 1024 becomes '1.0 KB', but 1536 becomes '1.5 KB'). Special attention will be paid to rounding behavior and ensuring consistent formatting across all unit boundaries.

ANSWER:
```javascript
function formatBytes(n) {
  if (n < 0 || isNaN(n)) return '0 B';
  if (n === 0) return '0 B';

  const units = ['B', 'KB', 'MB', 'GB', 'TB'];
  let value = n;
  let unitIndex = 0;

  while (value >= 1024 && unitIndex < units.length - 1) {
    value /= 1024;
    unitIndex++;
  }

  if (unitIndex === 0) {
    return `${Math.round(value)} ${units[unitIndex]}`;
  }

  const formatted = value.toFixed(1);
  return `${formatted} ${units[unitIndex]}`;
}

module.exports = formatBytes;
```

