I’ll design the DELETE endpoint behavior to align with REST conventions and business logic constraints. First, I’ll ensure proper HTTP status codes are returned: 204 No Content for successful deletion (no body needed), 404 Not Found when the order ID doesn’t exist, and 409 Conflict specifically for attempts to delete an already-shipped order—this prevents data integrity issues and clearly communicates the business rule violation. Edge cases include handling concurrent requests (e.g., two DELETEs for the same order where one ships it mid-flight), which should be resolved via optimistic locking or transactional checks, and ensuring the order ID is validated for format (e.g., UUID vs. integer) before querying the database to avoid unnecessary errors.

ANSWER:
export default {
  '/api/v1/orders/{id}': {
    DELETE: async (req, res) => {
      const { id } = req.params;
      if (!id || isNaN(id)) {
        return res.status(400).json({ error: 'Invalid order ID' });
      }
      const order = await db.orders.findByPk(id);
      if (!order) {
        return res.status(404).json({ error: 'Order not found' });
      }
      if (order.status === 'shipped') {
        return res.status(409).json({ error: 'Cannot delete an already-shipped order' });
      }
      await order.destroy();
      return res.status(204).send();
    }
  }
};

doc-013.text:
I’ll design a RESTful PUT endpoint for updating user information, focusing on three key aspects: (1) validation of the incoming name and email fields to ensure they meet basic criteria (e.g., non-empty, valid email format), returning 400 on failure; (2) enforcing authorization by checking if the authenticated user is allowed to edit the target user ID—returning 403 if they’re attempting to modify another user’s record; and (3) handling edge cases such as missing fields, malformed email addresses, or non-existent user IDs (though the spec only requires 400/403, I’ll assume a 404 for non-existent user is acceptable or handled upstream). The response body will include the updated user object on success (200), and error responses may include descriptive messages for clarity.

ANSWER:
const express = require('express');
const router = express.Router();

// Mock user database
const users = [
  { id: '1', name: 'Alice', email: 'alice@example.com' },
  { id: '2', name: 'Bob', email: 'bob@example.com' }
];

// Mock auth middleware
const authenticate = (req, res, next) => {
  // Simulate authenticated user ID from token/session
  req.authUserId = '1'; 
  next();
};

// Validation helper
const validateUserInput = (name, email) => {
  if (!name || typeof name !== 'string' || name.trim().length === 0) {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Name is required and must be a non-empty string.' };
  }
  if (!email || typeof email !== 'string' || !/^[^\s@]+@[^\s@]+\.[^\s@]+$/.test(email)) {
    return { valid: false, error: 'Valid email is required.' };
  }
  return { valid: true };
};

router.put('/api/v1/users/:id', authenticate, (req, res) => {
  const { id } = req.params;
  const { name, email } = req.body;

  // Authorization check: user can only edit their own record
  if (id !== req.authUserId) {
    return res.status(403).json({ error: 'Forbidden: You can only edit your own user profile.' });
  }

  // Validation
  const validation = validateUserInput(name, email);
  if (!validation.valid) {
    return res.status(400).json({ error: validation.error });
  }

  // Find and update user
  const userIndex = users.findIndex(u => u.id === id);
  if (userIndex === -1) {
    return res.status(404).json({ error: 'User not found.' });
  }

  const updatedUser = { ...users[userIndex], name: name.trim(), email: email.trim() };
  users[userIndex] = updatedUser;

  return res.status(200).json(updatedUser);
});

module.exports = router;

