When designing a retry mechanism, using exponential backoff helps prevent overwhelming a failing service by increasing the delay between retries after each failure. This approach reduces load on the system and gives it more time to recover, especially in cases of cascading failures or resource contention. Fixed delays can lead to repeated collisions (e.g., many clients retrying simultaneously), whereas exponential backoff—especially with jitter—spreads retries over time, improving overall system stability and success probability.

