I will design a health check endpoint that evaluates the application’s readiness by checking critical dependencies (e.g., database, cache, external services). The endpoint will return HTTP 200 only if all dependencies are healthy and the service is ready to accept traffic; otherwise, it will return HTTP 503 with a descriptive payload indicating which dependencies are unavailable. Edge cases include transient failures (e.g., brief network glitches), where the service may still be *starting up* or *recovering*, so I’ll ensure the health check is non-blocking and respects timeouts; also, I’ll avoid caching health status to guarantee real-time accuracy.

ANSWER:
const express = require('express');
const app = express();

// Simulated dependency health map
const dependencies = {
  database: true,
  cache: false,
  externalApi: true
};

// Health check endpoint
app.get('/healthz', (req, res) => {
  const unhealthyDeps = Object.entries(dependencies)
    .filter(([_, status]) => !status)
    .map(([name]) => name);

  if (unhealthyDeps.length === 0) {
    return res.status(200).json({ status: 'ok', dependencies: { ...dependencies } });
  }

  return res.status(503).json({
    status: 'unavailable',
    unhealthyDependencies: unhealthyDeps,
    message: `Service unavailable due to unhealthy dependencies: ${unhealthyDeps.join(', ')}`
  });
});

module.exports = app;

