I'll create a React Native class component that implements error boundary functionality using the componentDidCatch lifecycle method. This approach catches JavaScript errors anywhere in the child component tree, logs them, and displays a fallback UI instead of crashing the entire app. I need to handle edge cases like errors during the error boundary's own render method (which won't be caught), and ensure the fallback UI doesn't trigger infinite loops by avoiding state updates that might cause re-renders with the same error. Also, I'll make sure to properly clean up any references and handle cases where the error object might be undefined.

ANSWER:
